#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049643 :topic/id #uuid "078cdd1c-3c63-520c-a331-823f69f948c9" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110622 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110513 :question/id #uuid "6d75946e-ba1d-5f7b-b880-2f0ad83e5e4e" :question/math-academy-id "e-5161" :question/problem "Rebecca invested $\\$10,000$ into an account paying a $3\\%$ annual interest, compounded monthly. How much money will be in her account after $10$ years? Round your answer to the nearest dollar." :question/worked-solution "The compound interest equation states that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} P &= P_0\\left(1 + \\dfrac r n \\right)^{nt}. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are $12$ months in one year, so $n=12$. We also have an initial amount of $P_0=10,000$, an annual interest of $r=0.03$, and a length of $t=10$ years. Substituting these values into the formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} P &= P_0\\left(1 + \\dfrac {r} {n} \\right)^{(n)(t)} \\\\[5pt] &= 10,000\\left(1 + \\dfrac {0.03} {12} \\right)^{(12)(10)} \\\\[5pt] &= 10,000(1 + 0.0025)^{120} \\\\[5pt] &= 10,000(1.0025)^{120} \\\\[5pt] &= 10,000(1.349\\,353...) \\\\[5pt] &= 13,493.53... \\\\[5pt] &\\approx 13,494. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, after $10$ years Rebecca will have $\\$13,494$ in the account."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f42eb9ad-cd67-5669-a3d9-b464810319e5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Amount of Money After Compound Interest Is Applied"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110623 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110514 :question/id #uuid "3723a7d8-f191-548c-8c2d-d647aac03a48" :question/math-academy-id "e-5163" :question/problem "How much money would you need to deposit today at a $2\\%$ annual interest rate, compounded yearly, to have $\\$10,000$ in the account after $10$ years? Round your answer to the nearest dollar." :question/worked-solution "The compound interest equation states that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} P &= P_0\\left(1 + \\dfrac r n \\right)^{nt}. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the payments are compounded yearly, we have $n=1$. We also have an annual rate of $r=0.02$, and a final amount of $P=10,000$ after $t=10$ years. To find the initial deposit, $P_0$, we substitute these values into the formula and solve for $P_0$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} P &= P_0\\left(1 + \\dfrac {r} {n}\\right)^{nt} \\\\[5pt] 10,000 &=P_0\\left(1 + \\dfrac {0.02} {1}\\right)^{(1)(10)} \\\\[5pt] 10,000 &= P_0(1.02)^{10} \\\\[5pt] 10,000 &= P_0(1.218,994...) \\\\[5pt] P_0 &= \\dfrac {10,000} {1.218,994...} \\\\[5pt] P_0 &= 8,203.48... \\\\[5pt] P_0 &\\approx 8,203 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, you would need to deposit $\\$8,203$."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e04791d-e117-5a87-8491-1b2b5797f13a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Initial Deposit"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110624 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110515 :question/id #uuid "6367b497-624e-54b3-9890-0463c2c4ac7f" :question/math-academy-id "e-5164" :question/problem "If you deposit $\\$4,000$ dollars into an account with a $6\\%$ annual interest rate, compounded quarterly, how many years will it take until there is at least $\\$8,000$ in the account?" :question/worked-solution "The compound interest equation states that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} P &= P_0\\left(1 + \\dfrac r n \\right)^{nt}. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe annual interest is compounded quarterly, and there are $4$ quarters in one year, so $n=4$. We also have an initial amount of $P_0=4,000$ and annual rate of $r=0.06$.\n\nTo find the number of years it will take until\n\n$$\nP\\geq 8,000\n$$\n\nwe substitute these values into the formula and solve for $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} P = P_0\\left(1 + \\dfrac r n \\right)^{nt} &\\geq 8,000 \\\\[5pt] 4,000\\left(1 + \\dfrac {0.06} {4} \\right)^{4t} &\\geq 8,000 \\\\[5pt] (1 + 0.015)^{4t} &\\geq \\dfrac {8,000} {4,000} \\\\[5pt] 1.015^{4t} &\\geq 2 \\\\[5pt] \\log \\left(1.015^{4t}\\right) &\\geq \\log 2 \\\\[5pt] 4t\\log 1.015 &\\geq \\log 2 \\\\[5pt] 4t &\\geq \\dfrac {\\log 2} {\\log 1.015} \\\\[5pt] 4t &\\geq 46.56... \\\\[5pt] t &\\geq \\dfrac{46.56...}{4} \\approx 11.64 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRemember payments are compounded quarterly, so we must choose either $11.5$ or $11.75$ years.\n\n- After $11.5$ years, there is still less than $\\$8,000$ in the account, because $11.5<11.64$.\n- After $11.75$ years, there will be more than $\\$8,000$ in the account, since $11.75>11.64$.\n\nTherefore, it will take $11.75$ years ($11$ years and $3$ quarters) until there is at least $\\$8,000$ in the account."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2c88884a-128a-5d46-bfbe-3773139ee11c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Number of Years to Yield a Particular Amount"}] :topic/math-academy-id 1481 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049665} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051065}] :topic/title "Modeling With Compound Interest"}]}
